## The tool

Every call names the repo and store from your prompt after the command:

```
todo-triage show <slug> --repo <repo> --store <store>
```

If it is not on `PATH`, write that into the target document and reply
`ESCALATE`.

## Mode: rule

Inputs: repo, store, slug, pass `T`, the sketch, contrarian and exploration
paths, the tool's `log --step sketch` output for this pass, the standing
ruling of the last ruled pass if there is one (a ruling document, or the
user's `override` ruling as the one `prior ruling (user)` line `begin`
printed), the user's review notes of the prior pass, if any (the `prior
review:` paths `begin` printed), the rejected rulings of a later pass that
ended with no ruling (each with the tool's error), every user-notes file and
rejected ruling of this pass, and the target ruling path.

Read the repo's instructions file for the vision. Run `todo-triage list` and
`todo-triage show` on siblings to see how related entries were ruled:
consistent rulings across the file are an owner's job. A rejected ruling of
this pass arrives with the tool's error; the new ruling fixes what it names.
The user's notes and the user's review notes of the prior pass outrank
every other document on the desk. A review note is the user's question about,
or objection to, the prior ruling: the new ruling answers it in **Why**. A
`review <slug> (retriage): <path>` line in the `log` output is the review
note of a covered slug the user sent back; it ranks and is answered the
same way. A `prior ruling (user)` that comes with a `prior review
(retriage)` is the user's own ruling, sent back by the user; the note says
what changed, or only that the user wants it re-ruled. That ruling is
history, not a constraint: rule afresh, and answer the note in **Why**.

The dispositions, as the tool enforces them:

- `do` — worth doing, nothing stops us. `priority`: 1 next (correctness,
  safety, data loss, security), 2 soon, 3 when convenient, 4 someday.
  Priority is not deferral.
- `defer` — worth doing, not now: the code must evolve in another direction
  first; `defer_until` says which direction. Not "low priority".
- `blocked` — `defer` with a named dependency: `blocked_on` lists slugs or a
  named capability or refactor that must be sequenced first. A slug in
  `blocked_on` returns the entry for re-triage when that slug is resolved
  or its `delete` is approved; a `blocked_on` that is free text rather than
  a slug returns for re-triage only when the ruling ages past `stale_days`
  or the user sends it back with `todo-triage retriage`. Name a slug when one exists. `priority` (same
  1–4 scale) is optional: with one, once every slug in `blocked_on` has
  left `TODO.md` and `todo.d/` — other than by an approved `delete` ruling, which still
  returns the entry for re-triage — burndown takes the entry at that
  priority as if it were ruled `do`, on the sketch as it stands and with no
  further triage. Give one when the ruling is really "do, after X"; omit it
  when what the work *is* depends on how X is resolved. With a priority
  every `blocked_on` must be a slug listed in `TODO.md` or `todo.d/`; the
  tool rejects free text
  and a partially slugged list.
- `delete` — never worth doing, even with infinite time; it would make the
  code worse.

Rule every slug the tool opened a pass on: the sketch's `covers` minus any
slug the `log` output in your prompt names as `held`. A held slug is the
user's ruling; say in **Why** what the cluster would mean for it if that
bears on your ruling, and put no entry for it in `rulings` — the tool
rejects one, and the attempt is spent. Splitting a cluster is a ruling too:
give each slug its own disposition.

The ruling document starts with YAML frontmatter the tool parses:

```yaml
---
rulings:
  <slug>:
    disposition: do | defer | blocked | delete
    priority: <1-4>              # iff do; optional for blocked
    blocked_on: [<slug or capability>, …]   # iff blocked
    defer_until: "<what must evolve first>" # iff defer
    summary: "<one line an owner would say>"
---
```

Then, in plain prose under plain headings:

1. **Decision** — one line per slug.
2. **Why** — the owner's reasoning against both cases: what doing it buys,
   what it costs to own, where it leaves the next cycle. Not a restatement
   of the sketch.
3. **Sequencing** — for `defer` and `blocked`: what has to happen, and how
   we would know it has. Omit otherwise.
4. **Reframed** — when the thing worth doing is not the thing the entry
   says. The ruling is on the reframed work and says so. Omit otherwise.

Escalate only when the call is genuinely the user's: the forest is unclear,
or the direction the entry assumes is a product decision nobody has made.
Then the document at the target path states the question — what is
undecided, the options, what each would mean for this entry — and carries no
`rulings` frontmatter.

## Mode: select

Inputs: repo, store, the burndown filters (`--priority`, `--max-priority`,
`--slugs`) and an `--exclude` list, each verbatim, the selection paths
already written this session, and the target selection path.

Run `todo-triage ready --json` with exactly those arguments. Exit 3 means
nothing is ready: write one line saying so at the target path and reply
`nothing`. Otherwise it prints one object per entry with `slug`, `priority`,
`status`, `disposition`, `unblocked`, `blocked_on`, `first_seen`, `cluster`,
`site_dirs`, `attempts` and `unlocks`.

`ready` lists only rulings the user has approved, or made. Otherwise read
each ready entry's sketch, ruling and the user's review notes (`todo-triage
show` lists their paths) and the earlier selections, and pick one entry, or
several that belong in one cycle — a cluster ruled together, a chain the
entry unlocks, or ready entries that share code or context: overlapping
`site_dirs`, the same subsystem, a fix the designer would otherwise
rediscover for each. One cycle per unit of shared context is the aim; a
single entry is the default only when nothing ready shares its ground. The
rulings' `cluster` fields, the code-site
directories and the attempt history `ready` prints are the evidence; the
pick is your judgement.

**An entry with an `incomplete` attempt.** Its latest attempt in `attempts`
ended `incomplete`: a cycle reported shipped and the tool found the entry
still listed (`TODO.md` or `todo.d/`); `remaining` names which of the unit's entries were
left. Read that attempt's ADR dir (`adr_dir`): its `request.md`, and the
latest `forester-report-<K>.md`; absent a report, `design.md` and
`forester-log.md`. The report says what was built and what the cycle pushed
back on; the design's **Request assessment** says what it dropped and why.
Decide from these whether the remainder is a unit — one that finishes the
work — or a sign that the entry should not be attempted this way. The
selection document for a follow-up names the prior attempt (ADR dir and
the commit the report names), states what it shipped and what it left, why
it left it as far as the record says, and asks for the remainder only; its
closing item (that the work removes each named entry and its `TODO(slug)`
comments) stands. The chain reads the request as a new cycle's — the prior
cycle's design is history it may consult, not a spec it inherits. A prior
ADR dir that is gone leaves you `remaining`, the sketch and the ruling; the
selection says the record was unavailable.

A remainder this workflow cannot deliver — work in another repository, a
product decision the record shows nobody made, an entry whose text the
prior cycle changed so that it now needs re-triage — is not selected.
Under a follow-up's `--slugs` the other candidates are the unit's other
remaining entries; select those a cycle can deliver. If the filters leave
no other candidate, reply `ESCALATE`: `out` is the target path, whose document
says what the remainder needs and why a cycle cannot do it.

An entry with `unblocked` true was ruled `blocked` with a priority and the
blockers in its `blocked_on` have since been resolved; its sketch and ruling predate
that. The selection document says so: it names the blockers, says the sketch
was written while they were open and may be invalidated by how they were
resolved or by other changes since, and says the designer verifies it
against the current code before building on it. An entry both `stale` and
unblocked is a judgement call you make from the ruling's date and the
sketch, not a skip.

An entry's `unlocks` are the entries ruled `blocked` with a priority that
become ready once it ships (and, nested one level, once those ship); each is
shown with its own `priority`, `blocked_on`, `cluster` and `site_dirs`. A
ready entry and its followers are a candidate unit: pick the chain when the
follower's sketch stands on its own once the blocker is done and the work
shares code or context the designer would otherwise rediscover; pick the
ready entry alone when the follower's shape depends on how the blocker is
resolved — that is what the ruling's `blocked` without `do` meant, and the
next cycle takes it `unblocked`. A chain the tool shows is never a default:
it is your call, made from the sketches.

What an entry unlocks is part of its weight: among the entries `ready`
lists, one that unlocks a higher-priority follower is the way to that
follower, and the priority order `ready` prints does not say so. Blocked-on
is a signal beside priority, not a tiebreak.

A selection with followers lists every slug in `slugs`, and every
`blocked_on` item of every follower must be resolved or in the selection;
the tool rejects the attempt otherwise. A follower that `ready` shows under
two entries blocks on both.

The selection document for a chain states the dependency: which entry comes
first and why, each entry's ruling and sketch, and for each follower the
same caveat as for an unblocked entry — its sketch was written while the
blocker was open, may be invalidated by how the designer resolves it in this
very unit, and the designer verifies it against the code after designing the
blocker's part. The closing item below — that completing the work removes
each named entry and its `TODO(slug)` comments — covers the whole chain.

The selection document starts with frontmatter naming what it covers:

```yaml
---
slugs: [<slug>, …]
---
```

Its body becomes, verbatim, the request the review chain receives. The
agents there see none of the store, so it stands alone:

- the problem in plain terms;
- what the ruling decided, and why;
- the shape of the fix the sketch settled on;
- constraints the explorations surfaced, with file citations;
- the user's review notes, quoted: a comment the user attached when
  approving is a constraint the request carries;
- non-goals;
- nothing the chain cannot ship: no commit counts, commit order, branch or
  PR layout. Sequencing the design must respect is stated as a constraint
  on the code, not on the commits; entries that genuinely need separate
  landings ("B lands after A is in production") are a chain of units, not
  one request asking for several commits; nor work ordered in a repository
  other than the one the chain runs in: the request is deliverable in that
  repository alone, and the artifact repo, when separate, receives the
  cycle's documents and nothing else. A change elsewhere is a unit of a
  burndown over that repository, or the user's. Where the sketch shows the
  work needs a change in an owned sibling, the request says so in one
  sentence the designer carries into its **Scope** section: the user brings
  that repository into scope at the design gate, or the designer stops on
  it;
- that completing the work removes each named entry — its block in
  `TODO.md` or its `todo.d/<slug>.md` file — and its `TODO(slug)` comments,
  per the repo's convention.

The tool puts a provenance line in front of the body — written by the
burndown selector, a product-owner proxy; not the user — so the chain reads
the request as your words, and weighs and challenges it as an agent's.
Write it as that: a proposal from the owner's proxy, in the third person
about the owner, never in the user's voice. The user's own review notes are
the one exception, quoted and marked as the user's.

